Clockwork Orixá

Creative Catalyst: Instruments are forged from magnetized meteorite fragments and salvaged 18th-century clockwork mechanisms.

Clockwork Orixá is a frantic, polyrhythmic collision where the rigid, mechanical tension of Eurotrance is dismantled by the syncopated, earthly swing of Samba, all held together by the melancholic, repeating harmonic loops of the seventeenth-century Chacón. It sounds like a digital machine trying to learn how to dance in the rain: cold, metallic percussive hits ground the tracks, while high-velocity synth leads stutter against the natural resonance of meteorite-chime arrays and the frantic ticking of brass escapements.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 138–144 BPM; a hybrid "Ticking Samba" feel where the bass drum hits on the quarter note but the percussion ghost-notes slide behind the beat like a falling pendulum.
*   Harmonic Palette: Heavy reliance on the Phrygian dominant and chromatic minor scales; the music centers on a recurring four-chord ground bass (chacón) that repeats until the rhythm disintegrates.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Temporal Quantization Blur:* A technique where the MIDI grid is manually dragged to align with the erratic ticking of analog clockwork rather than a static click track.
       Ferrous Synthesis:* Passing digital oscillator signals through contact microphones attached to vibrating meteorite shards to impart a gritty, crystalline sustain.
       Non-Linear Echo-weaving:* Reverb tails that are physically mapped to a 3D space, causing sounds to "snag" on the mix edges like unraveled wool.
*   Dynamics: Violent, strobe-lit explosions of sound that abruptly collapse into solitary, ticking silence, mimicking a clock running out of spring tension.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the humid, subterranean clock-repair workshops of post-industrial Salvador, Bahia, Clockwork Orixá emerged when local DJs began modifying Roland drum machines with brass gears salvaged from colonial-era cathedral timepieces. The subculture—the "Gear-Keepers"—adorns themselves in intricate, woven copper filaments and raw, blackened asteroid dust, viewing every live set as a ritual of mending time itself. During the "Unspooling," a central ritual, the music peaks until the instruments physically overheat, at which point the dance floor falls into a communal, synchronized silence, listening for the internal rhythm of their own hearts.

Instruments: Meteorite-encrusted marimbas, brass-gear step-sequencers, liquid-cooled sawtooth synthesizers, and tension-calibrated clockwork snares.

For Fans Of: Autechre, Baden Powell, Early 90s Dutch Trance, Jordi Savall.

